course: dictionary
lesson: 10352
title: Plants lose water
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: Plants take in water through their roots, and it has to travel all the way up to the leaves. Much of it leaves the plant as water vapour, a gas. This loss of water from the leaves is called transpiration. «Plants give off water vapour from their leaves; this is called transpiration.»
explain: The water escapes through tiny holes in the leaves, called stomata. Most of these holes are on the underside of the leaf. «Leaves lose water vapour through tiny holes called stomata, mostly on the underside.» Because water keeps leaving, more water is pulled up from the roots.
explain: Context helps here too. If you read «transpiration pulls more water up from the roots», you can guess it moves water through a plant. Check your guess by swapping in the meaning: losing water through the leaves still makes sense.
fact: Transpiration means plants losing water as water vapour through tiny holes in their leaves.
fact: The tiny holes in leaves are called stomata.
fact: Transpiration is part of the water cycle, the way water moves between the ground, the air and back again.
fact: Scientists test transpiration by putting a plastic bag over a plant; after a while, water drops form inside the bag.
fact: In science, transpiration can also mean sweating in people and animals, but this use is rare.
exercise: What does transpiration mean? | The way plants make their own food using sunlight | The way roots soak up water from the soil | *The way plants lose water as water vapour through tiny holes in their leaves | The way seeds spread across the ground
exercise: «Leaves lose water vapour through tiny holes called stomata.» What are the tiny holes called? | *Stomata | Chlorophyll | Roots | Petals
exercise: «Transpiration pulls more water up from the roots.» Why does the water go up? | Because the stem is hollow and water falls in | *Because water keeps leaving the leaves, so more is drawn up | Because the leaves are too heavy to stay still | Because the roots push water out of the soil into the sky
test: Scientists cover a plant with a plastic bag to test transpiration. What do they look for? | Roots growing longer each day | Leaves turning bright red | *Water drops forming inside the bag
test: Where are most stomata found on a leaf? | *On the underside | On the top of the stem | On the roots
exam: Which sentence best explains transpiration? | Animals breathe out carbon dioxide into the air | *Plants give off water vapour from their leaves through tiny holes | Rain falls from clouds onto the hills | Plants eat soil to grow bigger and stronger
